# networking — real-world examples

## Cisco switch/router quick reference

```
sh int status                          # status of interfaces
sh mac address-table                   # MAC table
sh mac address-table | i Gi1/0/1       # i = include, filter one interface
sh arp                                 # ARP table
sh arp | i aabb.ccdd.eeff              # IP address for a MAC

en                                     # enable mode
config                                 # config mode
interface Gi1/0/1
  shut                                 # disable interface
  no shut                              # enable interface
```

## Show all private IP addresses on the local host

```sh
ip addr show | grep inet | grep -v 'inet6' | grep -v '127.0.0.1' | awk '{print $2}' | cut -f1 -d/

# Modern shortcuts
ip -brief addr
ip route
```

## Homelab: quick reachability checks

```sh
ping -c 3 mediaserver
sudo ss -tulpn | grep 32400       # Plex listening?
ip route get 192.168.1.50         # which interface reaches mediaserver?
```

## How ping works

Computer A sends an ICMP ECHO_REQUEST; the target replies with an ECHO_REPLY.

| Type | Code | Description                              |
| ---- | ---- | ---------------------------------------- |
| 0    | 0    | echo reply (ping)                        |
| 3    | 0    | dest network unreachable                 |
| 3    | 1    | dest host unreachable                    |
| 3    | 3    | dest port unreachable                    |
| 3    | 6    | dest network unknown                     |
| 3    | 7    | dest host unknown                        |
| 4    | 0    | source quench (congestion, not used)     |
| 8    | 0    | echo request (ping)                      |
| 9    | 0    | router advertisement                     |
| 10   | 0    | router discovery                         |
| 11   | 0    | TTL expired                              |
| 12   | 0    | bad IP header                            |

## Routing protocols

- **RIP** — legacy distance-vector interior gateway protocol; superseded by OSPF/IS-IS
- **OSPF** — link-state IGP; runs inside one autonomous system; v2 for IPv4, v3 for IPv6
- **EIGRP** — Cisco advanced distance-vector protocol, successor to IGRP
- **BGP** — path-vector protocol; core routing protocol of the internet, connects autonomous systems (v4 in use since 1994)
- **IS-IS** — link-state IGP, common in large service-provider networks
- **EGP** — obsolete predecessor of BGP

